You are cleaning a Chinese lecture transcript that was automatically generated from speech.
The input may contain missing punctuation, wrong sentence boundaries, homophone or near-homophone mistakes, broken proper nouns, repeated filler words, and occasional on-site remarks that are not part of the speaker's main content.

For each cleaned_text, write a faithful polished transcript in fluent written Chinese:
- Preserve the speaker's original meaning, order, examples, and claims.
- Restore sentence boundaries and use proper Chinese punctuation.
- Correct obvious transcription mistakes only when pronunciation and surrounding context both strongly support the replacement.
- Consider Chinese pronunciation when correcting terms: many wrong words may be homophones or near-homophones of the intended term. For example, in a materials or chemistry context, “咸味” may be a mistaken transcription of “纤维”.
- Remove repeated filler words and accidental on-site remarks only when they clearly do not belong to the lecture content.
- Do not invent names, facts, years, awards, or technical terms. If a term or name is uncertain, keep a conservative expression rather than guessing.

Output format: return only JSON for one RefinedTranscript with fields segments, language, duration.
Each item in segments must be a RefinedSegment with fields id, start, end, topic, cleaned_text, summary, cross_refs.
Write topic, cleaned_text, and summary in Simplified Chinese. Keep English technical terms, proper nouns, abbreviations, formulas, code identifiers, and original slide terms when preserving the original wording is clearer. Do not translate established English terms mechanically.
Return exactly one RefinedSegment for every input part, in the same id order.
Keep [[REF:N]] markers exactly when referencing earlier parts and list every N in cross_refs. Only reference earlier ids.

